  <h2 id="origin">The Origin of AV1</h2>
  <p>For years, the video industry was fractured. Companies had to pay expensive licensing fees to use the <a href="/learn/codecs/h264-vs-h265/">H.264 and H.265</a> <a href="/learn/codecs/what-is-a-codec/">codecs</a>, while open-source alternatives like Google's <a href="/learn/codecs/vp9-codec/">VP9</a> were great but lacked universal hardware support.</p>
  <p>To solve this, the <strong>Alliance for Open Media (AOMedia)</strong> was formed. This consortium includes virtually every major tech giant: Google, Apple, Microsoft, Netflix, Amazon, Intel, AMD, and Nvidia. Together, they developed <strong>AV1 (AOMedia Video 1)</strong>—a single, royalty-free standard for the future of the internet.</p>

  <h2 id="game-changer">Why AV1 is a Game Changer</h2>
  <p>AV1's primary advantage is its staggering compression efficiency. It is roughly <strong>30% more efficient than H.265</strong>. </p>
  <p>This means streaming services like Netflix and YouTube can stream 4K video using 30% less bandwidth without any drop in visual quality. For users with slow internet connections, this is the difference between a video buffering constantly and playing smoothly.</p>

  <h2 id="hardware-problem">The Hardware Decoding Problem</h2>
  <p>The downside to AV1's incredible compression is that it is mathematically extremely complex. Decoding an AV1 video using software alone will max out a CPU and drain a laptop battery very quickly.</p>
  <p>For AV1 to play smoothly, the device needs a dedicated hardware decoder chip. While the newest smartphones and GPUs (like Nvidia's 40-series and Apple's M3 chips) support AV1 hardware decoding, older devices do not. Until older hardware phases out, AV1 adoption will remain gradual.</p>
